---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  getSkillsForPhase,
  getSkillsForTrail,
  roadmapPhases,
  roadmapSkills,
  roadmapTrails,
} from '../../data/roadmap';
import { validateFieldNoteRefs, validateRoadmap } from '../../data/roadmap-validation';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const notes = await getCollection('notes', ({ data }) => !data.draft);
const noteMap = new Map(notes.map((note) => [note.id, note]));
const errors = [
  ...validateRoadmap(roadmapSkills, roadmapPhases, roadmapTrails),
  ...validateFieldNoteRefs(roadmapSkills, new Set(notes.map((note) => note.id))),
];
if (errors.length) throw new Error(`Invalid roadmap content:\n${errors.join('\n')}`);
---
<BaseLayout title="Self-taught developer roadmap" description="A practical, build-first roadmap for self-taught software developers.">
  <section class="roadmap-intro">
    <p class="kicker">start here</p>
    <h2>Self-taught developer roadmap</h2>
    <p>This is not a list of everything a software engineer could possibly know. It is a build-first path through the concepts that make it easier to create software, understand what it is doing, debug it when it breaks, and explain your decisions to someone else.</p>
    <div class="roadmap-principle"><strong>The loop:</strong> learn enough → build something → break something → debug it → explain what happened → repeat.</div>
    <p>You do not need to finish one phase perfectly before touching the next. Use the phases as orientation, then follow the problems your projects reveal.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="roadmap-status-key">
    <p class="kicker">a better definition of progress</p>
    <h2>Do not measure learning by pages read</h2>
    <p>For any topic, a useful progression is <strong>🌱 encountered → 🧪 practicing → 🔨 applied → 🧠 can explain → ✅ comfortable</strong>. The goal is not memorization. The goal is increasingly independent reasoning.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="roadmap-phases">
    {roadmapPhases.map((phase) => (
      <section class="roadmap-phase" id={`phase-${phase.id}`}>
        <div class="phase-number">{phase.number}</div>
        <div class="phase-content">
          <p class="kicker">roadmap phase</p>
          <h2>{phase.title}</h2>
          <p>{phase.summary}</p>
          <ul class="roadmap-note-list">
            {getSkillsForPhase(phase.id).map((skill) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{skill.title}</strong>
                <span>{skill.summary}</span>
                {skill.fieldNoteIds.length > 0 && <small>
                  field notes: {skill.fieldNoteIds.map((id, index) => {
                    const note = noteMap.get(id);
                    return note ? <><a href={`${base}notes/${note.id}/`}>{note.data.title}</a>{index < skill.fieldNoteIds.length - 1 ? ' · ' : ''}</> : null;
                  })}
                </small>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <section class="specialization-section">
    <p class="kicker">branch without losing the spine</p>
    <h2>Choose trails based on what you want to build</h2>
    <p>Trails reuse the same skills instead of creating separate curricula. A skill can belong to more than one trail.</p>
    <div class="trail-grid">
      {roadmapTrails.map((trail) => (
        <article class="trail-card">
          <h3>{trail.title}</h3>
          <p>{trail.summary}</p>
          <ul>
            {getSkillsForTrail(trail.id).map((skill) => <li>{skill.title}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="roadmap-next">
    <p class="kicker">the messy middle matters</p>
    <h2>Watch the roadmap get tested in real projects</h2>
    <p>The field notes are the durable lessons. The journey is where I document what I am learning now, including mistakes, blind spots, bugs, and the things I thought I understood until a project proved otherwise.</p>
    <p><a href={`${base}journey/`}>follow the learning journey →</a> · <a href={`${base}projects/`}>see the projects behind the notes →</a></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
